# Project Structure — App Router Segment Colocation

## Overview
This project is built with:
- Next.js (App Router)
- TypeScript (strict)
- Biome
- TailwindCSS
- Supabase

The core architectural principle is **route-segment colocation**.

> Each App Router route segment owns all UI, logic, types, and data-access code required for that route.

---

## Core Architecture: Route-Segment Colocation

- `src/app/<route>/` is the **boundary of a feature/page**
- All code related to a route must live inside its route segment directory
- No separate top-level `domains/` or `features/` directories are used

The App Router itself defines the feature boundaries.

---

## Canonical Route Structure

For a route segment `X` (e.g. `/mypage`):
```
src/app/X/
page.tsx
layout.tsx
loading.tsx        // optional
error.tsx          // optional

components/        // UI components used only by this route
api/               // data access & mutation helpers
domain/            // pure business rules (no React, no I/O)
hooks/             // route-specific hooks (client-only if needed)
types/             // types and schemas
constants/         // route constants (optional)
```
---

## Ownership Rules

- All code under `app/X/**` is owned exclusively by route `X`
- Code inside one route must **not** be imported by another route
- Cross-route reuse is forbidden via deep imports

If multiple routes require the same logic:
- Extract it into `src/shared/`
- Shared code must be truly generic and used by more than one route

---

## Role of App Router Files

- `page.tsx` and `layout.tsx` must remain thin
- They should:
  - compose components from sibling folders
  - avoid business logic and heavy data processing
- Business rules and data access belong in:
  - `domain/`
  - `api/`

---

## Import Rules

1. A route may freely import from its own subfolders
2. Cross-route imports are not allowed
3. Shared logic must live in `src/shared/`
4. Do not create implicit dependencies between routes

---

## Supabase Placement

- Supabase client factories live in:
  - `src/lib/supabase/server.ts`
  - `src/lib/supabase/client.ts`
- Route-level `api/` modules may call Supabase clients
- UI components must not directly access Supabase unless trivial and read-only

---

## Guiding Principle

> “The route is the feature boundary.”

If ownership is unclear, the code belongs to the route that renders it.